{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2185-09-24 20:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi, I'm Dr. Lee, one of your hospital doctors. I understand you came in because you’ve been feeling unwell?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2185-09-24 20:16:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, thank you for coming. It started this weekend—this strange 'wavy' feeling, like my whole body was off. Not dizzy, not lightheaded, just… unsteady."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2185-09-24 20:17:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I see. You mentioned it reminded you of how you felt before getting septic in the past. That’s a very valid concern, especially with your medical history."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2185-09-24 20:18:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Exactly. I’ve been through that before, and I don’t want to miss the early signs again. But my G-tube and femoral line look fine—no redness, no discharge."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2185-09-24 20:19:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good, that’s reassuring. The triage temperature of 100.4 was repeated and came back normal at 98.5, so no fever. Your labs, EKG, and urine were all unremarkable."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2185-09-24 20:20:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’m glad. But I’ve also had these episodes—head shaking, like nodding uncontrollably. I can’t stop it. It’s scary."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2185-09-24 20:21:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ve actually seen one of those episodes earlier. You were fully awake, answering questions, and your vitals stayed stable. That’s very important."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2185-09-24 20:22:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I was aware the whole time. It’s not like my old pseudoseizures—they were different. But I’m worried… my brother had glioblastoma. Could this be something like that?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2185-09-24 20:23:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I understand that fear. But your recent head CT was normal, and there’s no sign of a tumor. These episodes don’t look like seizures or anything malignant."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2185-09-24 20:24:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So what could it be? It feels so real when it happens."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2185-09-24 20:25:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It may be a benign movement issue—something like a tremor or myoclonus. We’ll set up a follow-up with your neurologist in two weeks to go over it in detail."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2185-09-24 20:26:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That helps. Thank you. I just want to make sure it’s not something dangerous."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2185-09-24 20:27:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Absolutely. We’ve also checked your abdomen. You have some tenderness, but your G-tube is working fine, and there’s no sign of infection or acute obstruction."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2185-09-24 20:28:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve been worried about this small lump in my lower right belly. It’s been there a while, but I keep thinking it could be something bad."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2185-09-24 20:29:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We felt that. It’s about 2 cm, soft, and not tender. Given your history of multiple surgeries, it’s most likely scar tissue. You already have an outpatient biopsy scheduled—good plan."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2185-09-24 20:30:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, that’s a relief. And my femoral line? I’ve had clots before."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2185-09-24 20:31:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Site looks clean, no redness or swelling. You’re on Lovenox 40 mg daily, which you tolerate well. We’ll keep that going."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2185-09-24 20:32:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Good. I bruise easily on higher doses, so this is what my doctor and I agreed on."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2185-09-24 20:33:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Makes sense. We’ve continued all your home meds—topiramate for migraines, levothyroxine, gabapentin, and the rest."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2185-09-24 20:34:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve had a few migraines lately too. Usually with visual aura. I’ve been using sumatriptan as needed."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2185-09-24 20:35:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll keep that available. Your current symptoms don’t seem to be migraine-related, but we’re managing them as part of your overall care."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2185-09-25 08:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. I reviewed your blood cultures—both came back with no growth. That’s excellent news—no sign of infection."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2185-09-25 08:16:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s such a relief. I was really worried about sepsis again."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2185-09-25 08:17:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Totally understandable. We also did orthostatics—your blood pressure stayed stable when you stood up. So not related to low blood pressure or dehydration."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2185-09-25 08:18:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And the head shaking? Did you see it again last night?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2185-09-25 08:19:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, the nurse and I both saw it. You were alert, talking, and vitals were normal. Still no signs of seizure activity."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2185-09-25 08:20:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’m so glad you saw it. I was afraid no one would believe me."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2185-09-25 08:21:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We believe you. It’s real, and we’re taking it seriously. But based on what we’ve seen, it’s not dangerous. Your neurologist will help figure out what’s causing it."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2185-09-25 08:22:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you. That means a lot."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2185-09-25 14:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re planning to discharge you tomorrow morning. You’ve been stable, labs are normal, and we’ve ruled out serious causes."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2185-09-25 14:31:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Already? I feel better, but I’m still nervous about the head shaking at home."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2185-09-25 14:32:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I understand. But remember, you’re safe. The episodes don’t affect your breathing or consciousness. And your neuro follow-up is already set."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2185-09-25 14:33:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay. And my G-tube and line? Should I watch for anything?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2185-09-25 14:34:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Keep an eye on the sites—redness, swelling, or drainage would be a reason to call. But they looked perfect today."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2185-09-25 14:35:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "What about the abdominal distention? The X-ray showed gassy bowels."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2185-09-25 14:36:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, there’s some distention, but no obstruction or free air. Likely related to your chronic motility issues. We’ll keep managing it as before—your TPN and meds."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2185-09-26 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good news—you’re cleared to go home. Physical therapy saw you this morning and says you’re safe to ambulate independently."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2185-09-26 09:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s great. I’ll follow up with my neurologist and get that nodule checked."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2185-09-26 09:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. And if you have any new symptoms—fever, severe pain, or changes in the shaking—don’t hesitate to call or come back."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2185-09-26 09:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I will. Thank you for listening and not dismissing my concerns."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 41,
      "time": "2185-09-26 09:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Of course. You know your body best. We’re here to support you, not ignore you. Take care, and I’ll see you in follow-up if needed."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2185-09-24 19:56:00",
    "admission_end": "2185-09-26 10:22:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "A woman with a complex medical history including sclerosing mesenteritis, chronic gastrointestinal dysmotility, and a history of recurrent DVTs was admitted due to a subjective feeling of being unwell and unsteady, accompanied by episodes of involuntary head nodding. She expressed concern these symptoms might signal sepsis or a neurological malignancy, especially given her family history. Evaluation revealed stable vital signs, normal labs, and no signs of infection at her G-tube or femoral line sites. Imaging showed bowel distention consistent with ileus but no acute obstruction. Two sets of blood cultures showed no growth. The head-shaking episodes were witnessed by the care team and determined to be non-epileptic, with the patient remaining alert and hemodynamically stable throughout. She was reassured, continued on all home medications including Lovenox for DVT prophylaxis, and discharged home with a planned neurology follow-up to evaluate the movement episodes and an outpatient biopsy for a benign-appearing abdominal nodule.",
    "problems": [
      "Sclerosing mesenteritis",
      "Migraine, unspecified, without mention of intractable migraine without mention of status migrainosus",
      "Gastrostomy status",
      "Personal history of pulmonary embolism",
      "Unspecified acquired hypothyroidism",
      "Sicca syndrome",
      "Other chronic pain"
    ]
  }
}
